Figure 1: A Σ-protocol for a language \emph{L}.

probabilistic-polynomial time. A \emph{decision problem} related to a
language \emph{L ⊆\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}consists in determining
whether a string \emph{x} is in \emph{L} or not. Given an instance
\emph{x}, we say that \emph{A} decides (or recognizes) \emph{L} if,
after a finite number of steps, the algorithm halts and outputs
\emph{A}(\emph{x}) = 1 if \emph{x ∈L}, otherwise \emph{A}(\emph{x}) = 0.
(Sometimes, we may call ``theorem'' a string belonging to the language
at hand.) We can associate to any NP-language \emph{L} a polynomial-time
recognizable relation \emph{RL} defining \emph{L} itself, that is
\emph{L} = \emph{\{x} : \emph{∃w} s.t. (\emph{x, w}) \emph{∈RL\}}, where
\emph{\textbar w\textbar{} ≤poly}(\emph{\textbar x\textbar{}}). The
string \emph{w} is called a \emph{witness} or \emph{certificate} for
membership of \emph{x ∈L}. For NP, \emph{w} corresponds to the
non-deterministic choices made by \emph{A}.

\textbf{Interactive protocols.} An interactive proof system (IPS) for
membership in \emph{L} is a two-party protocol, where a prover wants to
convince an efficient verifier that a string \emph{x} belongs to
\emph{L}. In a zero-knowledge interactive proof system, a prover
\emph{P} can convince a verifier \emph{V} that \emph{x ∈L} without
revealing anything beyond the fact that the statement is indeed true.
Informally, this means

that \emph{V} cannot exploit the interaction with \emph{P} for gaining
extra-knowledge. Such a property is formalized by requiring the
existence of an efficient algorithm \emph{S}, the \emph{zero-knowledge
simulator}, which produces messages indistinguishable from conversations
between an honest prover \emph{P} and a malicious verifier \emph{V∗}.
Besides the zero-knowledge property, any proof system satisfies two
standard requirements: proving true statements is always possible, while
it should be infeasible

to convince the verifier to accept a false statement as correct. These
two conditions are called

\emph{completeness} and \emph{soundness} respectively. Related to the
concept of interactive proof systems,

but even more subtle, is the notion of \emph{proof of knowledge}. In a
proof of knowledge (PoK), \emph{P} wants to convince \emph{V} that he
\emph{knows} a secret witness which implies the validity of some
assertion, and not merely that the assertion is true. To formalize the
fact that a prover actually ``knows

something'', we require that there exists an efficient algorithm
\emph{E}, called \emph{knowledge extractor}, that when given complete
access to the program of the prover can extract the witness.

\begin{quote}
An IPS or an interactive PoK is called \emph{public-coin} when the
verifier's moves consist merely
\end{quote}

of tossing coins and sending their outcomes to the prover. (In contrast,
in a \emph{private-coin} IPS

the verifier does not need to show the outcome of the coins to the
prover {[}28{]}.) We are mainly

interested in a specific class of public-coin interactive PoK systems
for NP-languages, called Σ-

protocols. Here, the parties involved share a string \emph{x} belonging
to a language \emph{L ∈}NP and the prover also holds a witness \emph{w}
for membership of \emph{x ∈L}. Thus, the prover \emph{P} wants to
convince the verifier \emph{V} that it ``knows'' a witness \emph{w} for
\emph{x}, i.e. that \emph{x} is in the language, without revealing the
witness itself. Σ-protocols have a 3-move shape where the first message
\emph{α}, called \emph{commitment},

is sent by the prover and then, alternatively, the parties exchange the
other messages \emph{β} and \emph{γ},

called (respectively) \emph{challenge} and \emph{response}. The
interaction is depicted in Figure 1. Besides

the standard properties held by any IPS, Σ-protocols satisfy a flavour
of zero-knowledge ---

called \emph{honest-verifier zero knowledge} (HVZK) --- saying that an
\emph{honest} verifier taking part in

the protocol does not learn anything beyond the validity of the theorem
being proven.

\textbf{Definition 1} (Σ-protocols)\textbf{.} \emph{A} Σ\emph{-protocol}
Σ = (\emph{P, V}) \emph{for an} NP\emph{-language L is a three-round
public-coin IPS where P} = (\emph{P}0\emph{, P}1) \emph{and V} =
(\emph{V}0\emph{, V}1) \emph{are PPT algorithms, with the following}

