\textbf{Definition 2} (Quasi unique responses)\textbf{.} \emph{A}
Σ\emph{-protocol has} quasi unique responses \emph{if for any}

\begin{quote}
\emph{PPT A and for any security parameter k it holds:}
\end{quote}

Prob{[}(\emph{x, α, β, γ, γ′}) \emph{←A}(1\emph{k}) : \emph{V}(\emph{x,
α, β, γ}) = \emph{V}(\emph{x, α, β, γ′}) = 1 \emph{∧γ ̸}= \emph{γ′}{]}
\emph{≈}0\emph{.}

\begin{quote}
A Σ-protocol has \emph{unique responses} if the probability above is
zero. The latter condition,

defined by Unruh in {[}41{]}, is also known as \emph{strict soundness}.

\textbf{Min-entropy of commitments.} Following {[}1, 2{]}, we use the
concept of min-entropy to

measure how likely it is for a commitment to collide with a fixed value.

\textbf{Definition 3} (Min-entropy of commitment)\textbf{.} \emph{Let k
be a security parameter and L be an} NP\emph{-language with relation RL.
Consider a pair} (\emph{x, w}) \emph{∈RL and let} (\emph{P, V}) \emph{be
an arbitrary three-round IPS. Denote with} Coins(\emph{k}) \emph{the set
of coins used by the prover and consider the}

\emph{set A}(\emph{x, w}) = \emph{\{P}0(\emph{x, w}; \emph{ρ}) : \emph{ρ
←}Coins(\emph{k}))\emph{\} of all possible commitments associated to w.
The min-entropy function associated to} (\emph{P, V}) \emph{is defined
as ε}(\emph{k}) = min(\emph{x,w})(\emph{−}log2 \emph{µ}(\emph{x,
w}))\emph{,where the minimum is taken over all possible} (\emph{x, w})
\emph{drawn from RL and µ}(\emph{x, w}) \emph{is the maximum probability
that a commitment takes on a particular value, i.e., µ}(\emph{x, w}) =
max\emph{α∈A}(\emph{x,w})(\emph{Prob}{[}\emph{P}0(\emph{x, w}; \emph{ρ})
=\emph{α} : \emph{ρ ←}Coins(\emph{k})){]})\emph{.}

