6

We say that (\emph{P, V}) is \emph{non-trivial} if \emph{ε}(\emph{k}) =
\emph{ω}(log(\emph{k})) is super-logarithmic in \emph{k}. Often, the
commitment is drawn uniformly from some set. In order for (\emph{P, V})
to be non-trivial, this set must have size exponential in \emph{k}.
Notice that most of natural Σ-protocols meet such a condition and, in
fact, non-triviality is quite easy to achieve, e.g. by appending
redundant random bits to the commitment.

\textbf{Forking lemma.} To prove our second main result, we make use of
the following version of the forking lemma, which appeared in {[}6{]}.

\textbf{Lemma 1} (General forking lemma)\textbf{.} \emph{Fix an integer
Q and a set H of size h ≥}2\emph{. Let} P \emph{be a randomized program
that on input y, h}1\emph{, . . . , hQ returns a pair, the first element
of which is an integer in the range} 0\emph{, . . . , Q and the second
element of which we refer to as a} side output\emph{. Let} IG \emph{be a
randomized algorithm that we call the input generator. The accepting
probability of} P\emph{, denoted} acc\emph{, is defined as the
probability that J ≥}1 \emph{in the experiment y ←}IG; \emph{h}1\emph{,
. . . , hQ ←H}; (\emph{J, s}) \emph{←}P(\emph{y, h}1\emph{, . . . ,
hQ})\emph{.}

\emph{The forking algorithm} FP \emph{associated to} P \emph{is the
randomized algorithm that on input y proceeds as follows.}

\emph{Algorithm} FP(\emph{y})\\
\emph{Pick coins ρ for} P \emph{at random}\\
\emph{h}1\emph{, . . . , hQ ←H}\\
(\emph{I, s}) \emph{←}P(\emph{y, h}1\emph{, . . . , hQ}; \emph{ρ})\\
\emph{If I} = 0 \emph{return} (0\emph{, ⊥, ⊥}) \emph{h′I, . . . ,
h′Q←H}\\
(\emph{I′, s′}) \emph{←}P(\emph{y, h}1\emph{, . . . , hI−}1\emph{, h′I,
. . . , h′Q}; \emph{ρ})\\
\emph{If} (\emph{I} = \emph{I′}) \emph{∧}(\emph{hI ̸}= \emph{h′I})
\emph{return} (1\emph{, s, s′}) \emph{else return} (0\emph{, ⊥, ⊥})

\emph{Let} ext = Prob{[}\emph{b} = 1 : \emph{y ←}IG; (\emph{b, s, s′})
\emph{←}FP(\emph{y}){]}\emph{, then} ext \emph{≥}acc \uline{acc}
\emph{Q−}\uline{1} \emph{.}

\textbf{3} \textbf{Properties of NIZKs in the Random Oracle Model}

\textbf{Removing interaction.} The Fiat-Shamir transform was originally
designed to turn three-round identification schemes into efficient
signature schemes. As Σ-protocols are an extension of three-round
identification schemes, it is not surprising that they can be considered
as a starting point for the Fiat-Shamir transform. The Fiat-Shamir
paradigm applies to any Σ-protocol (and more generally to any
three-round public-coin proof system): We start from an interactive
protocol (\emph{P, V}) and remove the interaction between \emph{P} and
\emph{V} by replacing the challenge, chosen at random by the verifier,
with a hash value \emph{H}(\emph{α, x}) computed by the prover, where
\emph{H} is a hash function modeled as a random oracle. Thus, the
interactive protocol (\emph{P, V}) is turned into a non-interactive one:
The resulting protocol, denoted (\emph{PH, VH}), is called
\emph{Fiat-Shamir proof system}.

Throughout this paper, we refer to the so called \emph{explicitly
programmable} random oracle model {[}42{]} (EPROM) where the simulator
is allowed to program the random oracle explicitly. We model this by
defining the zero-knowledge simulator \emph{S} of a non-interactive
zero-knowledge proof system as a stateful algorithm that can operate in
two modes: (\emph{hi, st}) \emph{←S}(1\emph{, st, qi}) takes care of
answering random oracle queries (usually by lazy sampling) while
(\emph{π, st}) \emph{←S}(2\emph{, st, x}) simulates the actual proof.
Note that calls to \emph{S}(1\emph{, · · ·} ) and \emph{S}(2\emph{, · ·
·} ) share the common state \emph{st} that is updated after each
operation.

7

\textbf{Definition 4} (Unbounded non-interactive zero
knowledge)\textbf{.} \emph{Let L be a language in} NP\emph{. Denote
with} (\emph{S}1\emph{, S}2) \emph{the oracles such that S}1(\emph{qi})
\emph{returns the first output of} (\emph{hi, st}) \emph{←S}(1\emph{,
st, qi}) \emph{and S}2(\emph{x, w}) \emph{returns the first output of}
(\emph{π, st}) \emph{←S}(2\emph{, st, x}) \emph{if} (\emph{x, w})
\emph{∈RL. We say a protocol} (\emph{PH, VH}) \emph{is a NIZK proof for
language L in the random oracle model, if there exists a PPT simulator S
such that for all PPT distinguishers D we have}

Prob{[}\emph{DH}(\emph{·})\emph{,PH}(\emph{·,·})(1\emph{k}) = 1{]}
\emph{≈}Prob{[}\emph{DS}1(\emph{·})\emph{,S}2(\emph{·,·})(1\emph{k}) =
1{]}\emph{,}

\emph{where both P and S}2 \emph{oracles output ⊥if} (\emph{x, w})
\emph{/∈RL.}

\begin{quote}
A well known fact is that, in the random oracle model, the Fiat-Shamir
transform allows to
\end{quote}

efficiently design digital signature schemes {[}22{]} and
non-interactive zero-knowledge protocols.

In fact, an appealing characteristic of this transform is that many
properties of the starting

protocol are still valid after applying it. In particular, it has been
proven that the Fiat-Shamir

transform turns any three-round public-coin zero-knowledge interactive
proof system into a

NIZK proof system {[}8{]}. It is straightforward to prove that the same
holds when the starting

protocol is ZK only with respect to a honest verifier, as stated in the
following Theorem.

\textbf{Theorem 1} (Fiat-Shamir NIZKs)\textbf{.} \emph{Let k be a
security parameter. Consider a non-trivial three-}

\emph{round public-coin honest-verifier zero-knowledge interactive proof
system} (\emph{P, V}) \emph{for a language L ∈}NP\emph{. Let H be a
function with range equal to the space of the verifier's coins. In the
random oracle model the proof system} (\emph{PH, VH})\emph{, derived
from} (\emph{P, V}) \emph{by applying the Fiat-Shamir transform, is
unbounded non-interactive zero-knowledge.}

\emph{sketch.} To prove that the proof system (\emph{PH, VH}) is
non-interactive zero-knowledge it is sufficient to show that there
exists a simulator \emph{S} as required in Definition 4. This can be
done by invoking the HVZK simulator associated with the underlying
interactive proof system. In particular, \emph{S} works as follows:

\begin{quote}
\emph{•} To answer query \emph{q} = (\emph{x, α}) to \emph{S}1,
\emph{S}(1\emph{, st, q}) lazily samples a lookup table \emph{TH} kept
in state \emph{st}. It checks whether \emph{TH}(\emph{q}) is already
defined. If this is the case, it returns the previously assigned value;
otherwise it returns and sets a fresh random value (of the appropriate

length).

\emph{•} To answer query \emph{x} to \emph{S}2 (respectively
\emph{S′}2), \emph{S}(2\emph{, st, x}) calls the HVZK simulator of
(\emph{P, V}) on input \emph{x} to obtain a proof (\emph{α, β, γ}).
Then, it updates \emph{TH} in such a way that \emph{β} =
\emph{TH}(\emph{x, α}). If \emph{TH} happens to be already defined on
this input, \emph{S} returns failure and aborts.
\end{quote}

