in question (i.e., for a false theorem). However, it is not clear
whether this condition still holds

\emph{after} the attacker observes valid proofs for adaptively chosen
(true or false) statements. The

notion of \emph{simulation soundness} deals with this case.

\textbf{Definition 5} (Unbounded simulation soundness)\textbf{.}
\emph{Let L be a language in} NP\emph{. Consider a proof system}
(\emph{PH, VH}) \emph{for L, with zero-knowledge simulator S. Denote
with} (\emph{S}1\emph{, S′}2) \emph{the oracles such}

8

\emph{that S}1(\emph{qi}) \emph{returns the first output of} (\emph{hi,
st}) \emph{←S}(1\emph{, st, qi}) \emph{and S′}2(\emph{x}) \emph{returns
the first output of}

(\emph{π, st}) \emph{←S}(2\emph{, st, x})\emph{. We say that} (\emph{PH,
VH}) \emph{is simulation sound with respect to S in the random}

\emph{oracle model, if for all PPT adversaries A the following holds:}

\begin{quote}
Prob{[}(\emph{x⋆, π⋆}) \emph{←AS}1(\emph{·})\emph{,S′}2(\emph{·}):
(\emph{x⋆, π⋆}) \emph{/∈T}

\emph{∧x⋆/∈L ∧VS}1(\emph{x⋆, π⋆}) = 1{]} \emph{≈}0\emph{,}
\end{quote}

\emph{where T is the list of pairs} (\emph{xi, πi})\emph{, i.e.,
respectively queries asked to and proofs returned by the simulator.}

We stress that the above definition relies crucially on the
zero-knowledge property of (\emph{PH, VH}), as we use a probability
experiment that defines a property of \emph{S} to define a property
about (\emph{PH, VH}). In particular the definition is most meaningful
for a simulator \emph{S} for which the sim-ulation of the random oracle
of \emph{S}1 is consistent with a truly random oracle \emph{H}. Also
note that \emph{S′}2allows \emph{A} to ask for simulated proofs of false
statements.

\begin{quote}
The possibility to request proofs of false statements has an interesting
consequence: simu-
\end{quote}

