\textbf{Simulation extractability.} Combining simulation soundness and
knowledge extraction, we

may require that even after seeing (polynomially) many simulated proofs,
whenever \emph{A} makes a new proof it is possible to extract a witness.
This property is called simulation extractability,

and implies simulation soundness. Indeed, if we can extract a witness
from the adversary's

proof even with small probability, then obviously the statement must
belong to the language

in question. We introduce a weaker flavor of simulation extractability
which we call \emph{weak}

\emph{simulation extractability}. The main difference with full
simulation extractability is that the

extractor \emph{EA} is now given complete control over the adversary
\emph{A}, meaning that it is allowed to rewind \emph{A} and gets to see
the answers of (\emph{S}1\emph{, S′}2). Moreover, we require that if
\emph{A} outputs an accepting proof with some probability, then
\emph{EA} can extract with almost the same probability.

\textbf{Definition 6} (Weak simulation extractability)\textbf{.}
\emph{Let L be a language in} NP\emph{. Consider a NIZK proof system}
(\emph{PH, VH}) \emph{for L with zero-knowledge simulator S. Let}
(\emph{S}1\emph{, S′}2) \emph{be oracles returning}

\emph{the first output of} (\emph{hi, st}) \emph{←S}(1\emph{, st, qi})
\emph{and} (\emph{π, st}) \emph{←S}(2\emph{, st, x})
\emph{respectively.} \emph{We say that}

(\emph{PH, VH}) \emph{is} weakly simulation-extractable \emph{with}
extraction error \emph{ν and with respect to S in the random oracle
model, if for all PPT adversaries A there exists an efficient algorithm
EA with access to the answers TH, T of} (\emph{S}1\emph{, S′}2)
\emph{respectively such that the following holds. Let:}

\begin{quote}
acc = Prob (\emph{x⋆, π⋆})
\emph{←AS}1(\emph{·})\emph{,S′}2(\emph{·})(1\emph{k}; \emph{ρ}) :
(\emph{x⋆, π⋆}) \emph{̸∈T} ; \emph{VS}1(\emph{x⋆, π⋆}) = 1

ext = Prob (\emph{x⋆, π⋆})
\emph{←AS}1(\emph{·})\emph{,S′}2(\emph{·})(1\emph{k}; \emph{ρ});

\emph{w⋆←EA}(\emph{x⋆, π⋆}; \emph{ρ, TH, T} ) : (\emph{x⋆, π⋆})
\emph{̸∈T} ; (\emph{x⋆, w⋆}) \emph{∈RL} \emph{,}
\end{quote}

\emph{where the probability space in both cases is over the random
choices of S and the adversary's random tape ρ. Then, there exist a
constant d \textgreater{}} 0 \emph{and a polynomial p such that
whenever}

acc \emph{≥ν, we have} ext \emph{≥}\uline{1} \emph{p}(acc
\emph{−ν})\emph{d.}

9

The above definition is inspired by similar notions in the context of
proofs of knowledge {[}5, 31, 41{]}. The value \emph{ν} is called
\emph{extraction error} of the proof system. We omit for better
readability that values acc\emph{,} ext\emph{, p, ν} all depend on the
security parameter \emph{k}. Note that a non-negligible extractor error
can be made exponentially small by sequential repetitions (see Appendix
C for a proof).

\textbf{Proposition 1} (Extraction error amplification)\textbf{.}
\emph{Let} (\emph{PH, VH}) \emph{be a weakly simulation ex-tractable
NIZK proof system with extraction error ν. Then, the proof system}
(\emph{P′H, V′H}) \emph{ob-tained by repeating sequentially} (\emph{PH,
VH}) \emph{for a number n of times yields a weakly simulation
extractable NIZK proof system with extraction error νn.}

It is useful to look at the relation between weak simulation
extractability and the following stronger property modeling
online-extraction.

\textbf{Definition 7} (Full Simulation extractability)\textbf{.}
