\textbf{4} \textbf{On the Non-malleability of the Fiat-Shamir Transform}

\textbf{4.1} \textbf{Simulation Soundness}

We now show that NIZK proofs obtained via the Fiat-Shamir transform from
any IPS of the public-coin type additionally satisfying the HVZK
property are simulation sound. Since Σ-protocols are a special class of
HVZK public-coin IPSs, we get as a corollary that Fiat-Shamir NIZK
proofs obtained from Σ-protocols are simulation-sound.

\textbf{Theorem 2} (Simulation soundness of the Fiat-Shamir
transform)\textbf{.} \emph{Consider a} non-trivial \emph{three-round
public-coin HVZK interactive proof system} (\emph{P, V}) \emph{for a
language L ∈}NP\emph{, with quasi unique responses. In the random oracle
model, the proof system} (\emph{PH, VH}) \emph{derived from} (\emph{P,
V}) \emph{via the Fiat-Shamir transform is a} simulation-sound
\emph{NIZK with respect to its canonical simu-lator S.}

\emph{Proof.} We assume that (\emph{PH, VH}) is a non-interactive
zero-knowledge proof system with the sim-ulator \emph{S} described in
the proof of Theorem 1, and show that (\emph{PH, VH}) is simulation
sound. We proceed by contradiction. Suppose there exists a PPT adversary
\emph{A} that breaks the simulation soundness of the non-interactive
protocol with non-negligible probability

