breaking the soundness of the underlying interactive scheme.
\emph{P∗}runs \emph{A} as a black-box and has to simulate its
environment by answering the queries to \emph{S}1 and \emph{S′}2in a
consistent way. More

precisely, \emph{P∗}works as follows. It guesses uniformly at random an
index \emph{j ∈}{[}\emph{Q}{]} and replies to queries to \emph{S}1 and
\emph{S′}2in the following way:

\begin{quote}
1. Answer query (\emph{xi, αi}) to \emph{S}1:

(a) Query 1 \emph{\sout{≤}i \sout{≤}j \sout{−}}1: Returns
\emph{H}(\emph{xi, αi}) if it is already defined; otherwise it samples

a random value \emph{βi} and sets \emph{H}(\emph{xi, αi}) := \emph{βi}.

(b) Query \emph{j}: Runs the protocol with the honest verifier \emph{V}
for statement \emph{xj}, using as a

commitment the value \emph{αj}. Obtains challenge \emph{βj} from
\emph{V} and program the oracle as

\emph{H}(\emph{xj, αj}) := \emph{βj}. The answer to \emph{A}'s query is
\emph{βj}.

(c) Query \emph{j} + 1 \emph{\sout{≤}i \sout{≤}Q}: Proceed as in Step
1a.

2. Answer query \emph{x} to \emph{S′}2: Run the HVZK simulator of the
interactive protocol on input

\emph{x} to obtain an accepting proof (\emph{α, β, γ}), and program the
oracle \emph{H} in such a way that

\emph{H}(\emph{x, α}) := \emph{β}. If the NIZK simulator returns
failure, which happens when \emph{H}(\emph{x, α}) is

already defined, output failure and abort, otherwise output (\emph{α,
γ}).

3. Answer \emph{V}'s challenge: Let \emph{x⋆,} (\emph{α⋆, γ⋆}) be the
instance and the proof output by \emph{A}. Return \emph{γ⋆}to \emph{V}
as the response to challenge \emph{βj} in step 1b.
\end{quote}

We need to estimate the probability that \emph{P∗}succeeds in breaking
the soundness of the interactive scheme (\emph{P, V}) in terms of the
probability that \emph{A} outputs an accepting proof (\emph{α⋆, γ⋆}) for
a false statement \emph{x⋆}. Suppose that (\emph{x⋆, α⋆}) has been asked
to the random oracle as the \emph{j⋆}-th query

and we have \emph{j} = \emph{j⋆}, i.e., \emph{P∗}guesses the correct
index for which \emph{A} outputs an accepting proof for a false
statement \emph{x⋆}. In such a case, \emph{P∗}breaks the soundness of
(\emph{P, V}). Hence, we get:

\begin{quote}
Prob{[}\emph{P∗}wins{]} = Prob{[}\emph{A} wins \emph{∧j} =
\emph{j⋆∧}proof{]}
\end{quote}

= Prob{[}\emph{A} wins \emph{∧}proof{]} \emph{·} Prob{[}\emph{j} =
\emph{j⋆}{]}\emph{,}

where the second equality comes from the fact that \emph{P∗}guesses
\emph{j⋆}correctly indipendently of the

event that \emph{A} is successful and proof happens. Since the index
\emph{j} is chosen at random in {[}\emph{Q}{]}, we have
Prob{[}\emph{P∗}wins{]} = \emph{Q·}Prob{[}\emph{A} wins
\emph{∧}proof{]}. Whenever \emph{P∗}wins, it breaks the soundness of

the interactive scheme: by hypothesis, this happens only with negligible
probability. Therefore:

Prob{[}\emph{A} wins \emph{∧}proof{]} = \emph{Q ·}
Prob{[}\emph{P∗}wins{]} \emph{≤negl}(\emph{k})\emph{.}

Now we can bound the probability that \emph{A} succeeds. As we assume,
\emph{A} breaks the simulation

soundness of the scheme with non-negligible probability \emph{ϵ}:

Prob{[}\emph{A} wins{]} \emph{≤}Prob{[}\emph{A} wins \emph{∧}proof{]} +
Prob{[}\emph{A} wins \emph{∧}proof{]} \emph{≤negl}(\emph{k})\emph{,}

