Applying the Naor-Yung paradigm, consider the language:

\begin{quote}
\emph{L} = (\emph{c, pk, c′, pk′}) : \emph{∃r, r′∈}Z\emph{q, m ∈}G s.t.

\emph{c} = (\emph{gr} 1\emph{, . . . , grℓ, hr · m})\emph{, c′} =
(\emph{gr′}1\emph{, . . . , gr′ℓ, h′r′ · m}) \emph{,}
\end{quote}

where \emph{c} = (\emph{c}1\emph{, . . . , cℓ}+1) and \emph{c′}=
(\emph{c′}1\emph{, . . . , c′ℓ}+1) are BHHO encryptions with randomness
\emph{r} and \emph{r′}, using public keys \emph{pk} = (\emph{g}1\emph{,
. . . , gℓ, h}) and \emph{pk′}= (\emph{g}1\emph{, . . . , gℓ, h′})
respectively. The pair \emph{w} = (\emph{r, r′}) is a witness for a
string \emph{x} = (\emph{c, pk, c′, pk′}) \emph{∈L}. Consider the
following interactive protocol Σ = (\emph{P, V}) for the above
language:\\
1. \emph{P} chooses \emph{s, s′}at random from Z\emph{q} and computes
the commitment:

\begin{quote}
\emph{α} = ((\emph{α}1\emph{, ..., αℓ})\emph{,} (\emph{α′}1\emph{, ...,
α′ℓ})\emph{, α′′}) = ((\emph{gs} 1\emph{, ..., gsℓ})\emph{,}
(\emph{gs′}1\emph{, ..., gs′ℓ})\emph{, hs ·}
(\emph{h′})\emph{s′})\emph{.}

\$ 2. The verifier \emph{V} chooses a random challenge
\emph{β←}Z\emph{q}.

3. The prover computes the response \emph{⃗γ} = (\emph{γ, γ′}) =
(\emph{s −β · r, s′}+ \emph{β · r′}).

4. Given a proof \emph{π} = (\emph{⃗α, β,⃗γ}), the verifier \emph{V}
checks that:\\
(\emph{α}1\emph{, . . . , αℓ}) = (\emph{gγ}1\emph{· cβ}1\emph{, . . . ,
gγℓ· cβℓ})\\
(\emph{α′}1\emph{, . . . , α′ℓ}) = (\emph{gγ′}1\emph{·}
(\emph{c′}1)\emph{−β, . . . , gγ′ℓ·} (\emph{c′ℓ})\emph{−β}) \emph{α′′}=
\emph{hγ·} (\emph{h′})\emph{γ′·} (\emph{cℓ}+1 \emph{·}
(\emph{c′ℓ}+1)\emph{−}1)\emph{β.}
\end{quote}

In Appendix D.4 we prove that the above protocol is a Σ-protocol for the
language \emph{L}. With the Naor-Yung paradigm applied to the BHHO
encryption scheme we get a ciphertext (\emph{c, c′, π}) consisting of
4\emph{ℓ}+ 3 elements in G plus 2 elements in Z\emph{q}. Moreover, the
fact that the BHHO encryption scheme is CPA-secure against key leakage
together with the result of Naor-, show that the above instantiation is
CCA-secure against key-leakage attacks.

\textbf{Corollary 1.} \emph{Let k be a security parameter. Assuming that
the DDH problem is hard in} G\emph{, the Naor-Yung paradigm applied to
the BHHO encryption scheme yields an encryption scheme that}

