Key-dependent message (KDM) security of a public-key encryption scheme
requires that the scheme remains secure even against attackers allowed
to see encryptions of the value \emph{f}(\emph{sk}), where \emph{f ∈F}
for some class of functions \emph{F}.

Camenisch, Chandran and Shoup {[}14{]} show that a variation of the
Naor-Yung paradigm instantiated with a simulation-sound NIZK can still
leverage CPA-security to CCA-security, even in the context of KDM
security. We revisit their proof in the random oracle model in Appendix
D.5. Also in this case, only \emph{simulation soundness} is needed for
the proof.

the scheme Π\emph{′′}obtained by applying the Naor-Yung paradigm to
(Π\emph{,} Π\emph{′}) --- i.e., an encryption Roughly, for some function
family \emph{F}, if Π is KDM{[}\emph{F}{]}-CPA secure and Π\emph{′}is
CPA-secure,

of \emph{m ∈M} is a tuple \emph{c′′}= (\emph{c, c′, π}) where \emph{c}
encrypts \emph{m} under Π, \emph{c′}encrypts \emph{m} under Π\emph{′}and
\emph{π}

16

is a simulation-sound NIZK proof that \emph{c} and \emph{c′}encrypt the
same message --- is KDM{[}\emph{F}{]}-CCA secure.

Let \emph{ski}{[}\emph{j}{]} denote the \emph{j}-th bit of \emph{ski}.
The BHHO encryption scheme was the first KDM-CPA secure encryption
scheme, with respect to the class of all projection functions \emph{F↓}=
\emph{F}read \emph{∪F}flip, where

\begin{quote}
\emph{F}read = \emph{fi,j} :\emph{⃗sk →ski}{[}\emph{j}{]} \emph{i,j}and
\emph{F}flip = \emph{fi,j} :\emph{⃗sk →}1 \emph{−ski}{[}\emph{j}{]}
\emph{i,j.}
\end{quote}

More generally, when the message space is a linear space over Z\emph{q},
we define the function class \emph{PJ} (\emph{F↓}) as the class of all
affine combinations of elements in \emph{F↓}.

Now we can instantiate the general transform of {[}14{]} as follows. We
choose Π to be BHHO,Π\emph{′}to be ElGamal (say with \emph{pk′}=
\emph{h′}= \emph{gz′}1) and we build a Σ-protocol Σ\emph{′} for the
Naor-Yung language relative to Π and Π\emph{′}. Protocol Σ\emph{′}can be
easily derived from protocol Σ of the last section, by just compressing
the commitment as in \emph{⃗α} = ((\emph{gs} 1\emph{, . . . ,
gsℓ})\emph{, gs′}1\emph{, hs ·} (\emph{h′})\emph{s′}) (and simplifying
the verification procedure accordingly). Hence, Theorem 2 yields the
following result.

\textbf{Corollary 2.} \emph{Assuming that the DDH problem is hard in}
G\emph{, the Naor-Yung paradigm in-stantiated with BHHO and ElGamal
encryption schemes yields a} KDM{[}\emph{PJ} (\emph{F↓}){]}-CCA
\emph{secure encryption scheme in the random oracle model. An encryption
consists of ℓ}+ 3 \emph{elements in} G \emph{plus} 3 \emph{elements in}
Z\emph{q.}

\textbf{Beyond Naor-Yung.} Another paradigm that yields
chosen-ciphertext security from NIZKs, based on \emph{proving knowledge
of the plaintext}, was suggested by Rackoff and Simon {[}38{]}. Such a
construction is somewhat more natural and more efficient than the
twin-encryption paradigm: a message \emph{m} is encrypted (only once)
under a CPA-secure encryption scheme, and a NIZK proof of knowledge of
the plaintext is attached to the ciphertext. However, (to the best of
our knowledge) truly efficient constructions for sufficiently strong
NIZK proofs of knowledge are not available even using random oracles.
One can hope that using the weaker from of extractability afforded by
the Fiat-Shamir transform one could at least obtain NM-CPA secure
encyption, and this is indeed what is aimed at in the ongoing work of
{[}9{]}.

