\textbf{A Defining Proofs of Knowledge}

Many different definitions of proof of knowledge have been proposed in
the literature. According to Unruh {[}41{]}, it is possible to identify
three main distinct notions, which he refers to as A, B and C-style
PoKs.

\textbf{Definition 8} (PoK)\textbf{.} \emph{Let ν} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,}
1\emph{\}∗→}{[}0\emph{,} 1{]} \emph{be a function and R be a binary
relation, with corresponding language LR. A protocol} (\emph{P, V})
\emph{is a} proof of knowledge \emph{for R, with} knowledge error
\emph{ν if the following properties hold:}

\textbf{Completeness.} \emph{For any pair} (\emph{x, w}) \emph{∈R, if P
and V follow the protocol specification on public input x and private
input w (held by P), then the verifier accepts.}

\textbf{Validity.} \emph{There exist a probabilistic oracle machine E
and a constant c \textgreater{}} 0 \emph{such that, for any x ∈LR,
whenever a prover P∗convinces V on common input x with
probabilityϵ}(\emph{\textbar x\textbar{}}) \emph{\textgreater{}
ν}(\emph{\textbar x\textbar{}})\emph{, then the extractor EP∗, taking x
as an input and having oracle access to P∗, outputs a witness w such
that R}(\emph{x, w}) = 1\emph{,}

