The HVZK property essentially says there exists a simulator \emph{S}
that produces, on input \emph{x}, transcripts that are indistinguishable
from real conversations between \emph{P} and \emph{V} sharing input
\emph{x}, as long as \emph{V} behaves honestly. It is possible to give
stronger flavors of this property. A first example is the case of
\emph{special honest-verifier zero-knowledge} (SHVZK), as introduced by
Cramer, Damg˚ard and Schoenmakers {[}17{]}. Intuitively, this property
says that the simulator \emph{S} can take \emph{any β} as input and
produce a conversation indistinguishable from the space of all
conversations between honest parties in which \emph{β} is the challenge.
To formalize this, the experiments of Definition 1 are modified in such
a way that both the verifier and the simulator get also \emph{β} as
input. Even though the S-HVZK property is seemingly stronger than the
plain HVZK property, the two notions are essentially equivalent. In
fact, as Fischlin showed in {[}23{]}, any Σ-protocol where the challenge
has size logarithmic in the security parameter, satisfies the stronger
requirement of S-HVZK.

A stronger variant of zero-knowledge --- first introduced by Bellare and
Ristov {[}7{]} --- is \emph{strong special honest-verifier
zero-knowledge} (SS-HVZK), which differs from the previous one in the
fact that the simulator has to produce indistinguishable triplets
(\emph{α, β, γ}) where the string \emph{γ} can be chosen at random and
\emph{S} can compute the commitment \emph{α} trough a
\emph{deterministic} function \emph{φ} of

