\emph{f} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}be a
one-way function. We start with an arbitrary Σ-protocol Σ = (\emph{P,
V}) which satisfies the SS-HVZK property. The protocol Σ\emph{′}=
(\emph{P′, V′}) is essentially identical to Σ, however the prover now
also samples a random \emph{r} and adds \emph{r} to the commitment
\emph{α}; moreover it also appends \emph{z} = \emph{f}(\emph{r}) to the
last message. The verifier \emph{V′}runs the verifier \emph{V}, but also
checks that \emph{z} = \emph{f}(\emph{r}).

\textbf{Proposition 2} (Separation between S-HVZK and SS-HVZK)\textbf{.}
\emph{Assuming the existence of one-way functions, there exists a}
Σ\emph{-protocol which is} S-HVZK \emph{but not} SS-HVZK\emph{. More
precisely, let} Σ = (\emph{P, V}) \emph{be a} Σ\emph{-protocol which is}
SS-HVZK \emph{and f be a one-way function. Then, the protocol}
Σ\emph{′}= (\emph{P′, V′}) \emph{of Figure 2 is} S-HVZK \emph{but not}
SS-HVZK\emph{.}

\emph{Proof.} We show that the protocol Σ\emph{′}cannot satisfy the
stronger SS-HVZK property, even if the original protocol Σ does. The
intuition for this is that, if it could be possible to compute the
commitment \emph{α′after} having chosen the challenge \emph{β} and the
response \emph{γ′}, then it could also be possible to invert \emph{f}.
We now turn this intuition into a formal proof. Assume, towards a

