\textbf{D.2}

\begin{quote}
Prob{[}ExpLKG-ATK Π\emph{,A} (1\emph{k}) = 1{]} \emph{≤}1 2 +
\emph{negl}(\emph{k})\emph{.}

\textbf{Proof of Theorem 4}
\end{quote}

This proof follows the same strategy given in {[}19{]}, Theorem 4.3,
with minor changes. We consider the following two games:

\textbf{Game 0:} This is the unforgeability game of Definition 10
tailored for leakage-resilient signa-tures described in 5.1.

\textbf{Game 1:} In this game, we change the way in which the signing
oracle answers to \emph{A}'s queries. Instead of giving valid proofs
\emph{πi}, it answers signature queries \emph{mi} with simulated proofs
\emph{S}(\emph{pk, mi}). Notice that we use a simplified notation and
denote \emph{S} the simulator that combines \emph{S}1 (which answers
queries to the random oracle) and \emph{S}2 (which generates the proofs)
from Definition 4.

In Game 1, the winning condition of \emph{A} is still to compute a valid
forgery, i.e., to compute (\emph{m⋆, σ⋆}) such that Vrf(\emph{pk, m⋆,
σ⋆}) = 1. The transition from Game 0 to Game 1 is based on the
indistinguishability between proofs produced by the prover and proofs
computed by the simulator. For the unbounded non-interactive
zero-knowledge property of the scheme, the success probability in the
two games turns out to be negligibly close. Indeed, the adversary does
not notice whether she is talking to the signing oracle Sign\emph{sk} or
to the simulator \emph{S}. Suppose for sake of contradiction that the
success probability of \emph{A}, that we denote \emph{ϵ}, is
non-negligible (wlog, we bound the success probability of \emph{A} for
Game 1).

25

\begin{quote}
We next construct an adversary \emph{B} that runs \emph{A} as a
