We stress that, even if not explicitly written (for better readability),
the leakage functions \emph{f} may depend on the random oracle \emph{H}.
However, such a dependence does not give any significant advantage to
the adversary. In what follows, we derive a series of games whose
outcomes cannot be mutually distinguished, as long as one does not
violate the hypothesis of the theorem.

27

\textbf{Game 0.} We consider the LKG-CCA experiment defined above, in
the case the random bit is \emph{b} = 0. Observe that the challenge
ciphertext (\emph{cb, c′b, π}) contains encryptions of the same message
\emph{m}0.

\textbf{Game 1.} This game differs from the previous one in that the
proof \emph{π} attached to the challenge ciphertext is not properly
generated by the prover, but computed by the ZK simulator.

\textbf{Game 2.} As in Game 1, but in the challenge phase the ciphertext
\emph{c′}Enc\emph{′}(\emph{pk′, m}1). As a consequence, the attached
proof \emph{π} is a fake one, since \emph{cb} and \emph{c′} \emph{b}is
computed as \emph{c′b←} \emph{b} encrypt \emph{m}0 and \emph{m}1
respectively.

\textbf{Game 3.} This game is defined as Game 2 but the decryption
oracle uses the secret key \emph{sk′}instead of \emph{sk}, i.e. answers
to queries (\emph{c, c′, π}) by computing Dec\emph{′}(\emph{sk′, c′}).

\textbf{Game 4.} Again, we change the encryption oracle in such a way
that the first ciphertext \emph{c} encrypts the message \emph{m}1. This
means that the challenge ciphertext contains Enc(\emph{pk, m}1) and
Enc\emph{′}(\emph{pk′, m}1), both encryptions of the same message
\emph{m}1, hence it is a valid ciphertext.

\textbf{Game 5.} As in Game 4, but we restore the prover \emph{PH}to
compute proofs.

\textbf{Game 6.} Game 6 differs from the previous one in that the
decryption oracle restart to invoke Dec(\emph{sk, ·}) on input the first
ciphertext \emph{c} (and the leakage oracle also uses \emph{sk}). This
is the experiment LKG-CCA for \emph{b} = 1.

Denote by \emph{Si} the adversary success in Game i, for \emph{i} =
0\emph{,} 1\emph{, . . . ,} 6. Observe that
\emph{\textbar{}}Prob{[}\emph{S}0{]}
\emph{−}Prob{[}\emph{S}6{]}\emph{\textbar{}} bounds the advantage of a
(\emph{λ}-key-leakage) CCA-adversary attacking the scheme Π\emph{∗}. We
show that the difference \emph{\textbar{}}Prob{[}\emph{Si}{]}
\emph{−}Prob{[}\emph{Si}+1{]}\emph{\textbar{}} is negligible for any
\emph{i} = 1\emph{, . . .} 5, which implies the former.

0 \emph{→}1 The transition between games 0 and 1 is based on the
indistinguishability of NIZK proofs properly computed from proofs
generated by the simulator. Indeed, if the probabilities of winning in
the two games were not negligibly close, one could build an efficient
distin-guisher able to detect whether a proof comes from the prover
\emph{PH}or from the simulator \emph{S}. 1 \emph{→}2 CPA-security of the
encryption scheme Π\emph{′}means that no efficient adversary can
distin-guish between encryptions of different messages under the same
public key. In particular, encryption of different messages under the
same public key \emph{pk′}are indistinguishable.

\begin{quote}
\emph{b, π}) is generated: Game 1 and Game 2 differ only in the way the
challenge ciphertext (\emph{cb, c′}\\
ciphertext \emph{c′b}equals Enc\emph{′}(\emph{pk′, m}0) in the first
case, while in Game 2 it is Enc\emph{′}(\emph{pk′, m}1).

Thus, any PPT distinguisher who recognizes which games is involved with
could be di-rectly used to discern which one of the two plaitexts has
been encrypted.
\end{quote}

2 \emph{→}3 The difference between Game 2 and Game 3 lies in the way the
decryption oracle answers adversarial queries (\emph{c, c′, π}), namely
by decrypting \emph{c} with key \emph{sk} or \emph{c′}with key
\emph{sk′}, respec-tively. The only chance to tell apart the two
experiments is to query the decryption oracle with an invalid ciphertext
(\emph{c, c′, π}) such that \emph{c} and \emph{c′}are the encryptions of
different mes-sages. This would require to produce an accepting proof
\emph{π⋆}for a pair (\emph{c, c′}) \emph{/∈LNY} (the decryption oracle
checks the validity of the proof before allowing decryption). Observe
that in both games, the adversary actually receives a fake (simulated)
accepting proof for a false statement (since one of the ciphertext
encrypts \emph{m}0 and the other encrypts \emph{m}1), and she might
potentially gain some information from that proof. However, the
simulation soundness of the proof system forbids her to produce a new
accepting proof for a false statement.

28

3 \emph{→}4 The transition between Game 3 and Game 4 is equivalent to
the one involving games 1 and 2, that we have already discussed. Since
the scheme Π is CPA-secure, indistin-

\begin{quote}
guishability of ciphertexts Enc(\emph{pk, m}0) and Enc(\emph{pk, m}1)
implies that the probabilities of

succeeding in the two games are negligibly close.
\end{quote}

4 \emph{→}5 Another symmetry makes similar the transition from Game 4 to
Game 5 with the one between games 0 and 1: indistinguishability follows
from the zero-knowledge property of

\begin{quote}
the non-interactive proof system.
\end{quote}

5 \emph{→}6 Game 6 differs from Game 5 in that the decryption oracle
restarts to decrypt ciphertext \emph{c} by using secret key \emph{sk},
as in the original experiment. To notice the difference, an efficient

\begin{quote}
distinguisher should be able to produce an invalid ciphertext (\emph{c,
c′, π}), hence an accepted

proof \emph{π} for a false statement, as for the transition between
games 2 and 3. We have

already seen that no adversary is allowed to do so, otherwise the
simulation soundness of

the proof system would be broken. Moreover, in games 5 and 6, the
challenge ciphertext

contains two encryptions of the same message \emph{m}1, that means it is
a valid ciphertext.

The adversary is not even allowed to get fake simulated proof, thus
producing fresh fake

proofs would in fact violate the soundness property of the proof system.
\end{quote}

