\textbf{Game 0.} Is the KDM-CCA experiment defined above, when \emph{b}
= 0.

\textbf{Game 1.} This game differs from the previous one in that the
proof \emph{π} and queries to the random oracle are not computed using
\emph{PH}and \emph{H} respectively, but are produced by the ZK
simulator. \emph{This is indistinguishable by the unbounded
zero-knowledge property of the proof system.}

\textbf{Game 2.} As in Game 1, but encryption queries for index \emph{i}
are answered by computing the \emph{i,} 0\emph{\textbar f}(
\emph{⃗sk})\emph{\textbar{}}). (Hence, the attached proof \emph{π} is a
simulated second ciphertext as \emph{c′←}Enc\emph{′}(\emph{pk′}proof of
a false statement.) \emph{This is indistinguishable due to the
CPA-security of} Π\emph{′.}

\textbf{Game 3.} This game is defined as Game 2 but decryption queries
for index \emph{i} are answered by using secret key \emph{sk′i}instead
of \emph{ski}, i.e. answer to queries (\emph{i,} (\emph{c, c′, π})) by
comput-ing Dec\emph{′}(\emph{sk′i, c′}) whenever \emph{π} is accepted.
\emph{This is indistinguishable due to the simulation soundness of the
proof system.}

\textbf{Game 4.} We change the encryption oracle in such a way that the
first ciphertext is computed as \emph{c ←}Enc(\emph{pki,}
0\emph{\textbar f}( \emph{⃗sk})\emph{\textbar{}}). \emph{This is
indistinguishable due to the KDM-CPA security of} Π\emph{.} \textbf{Game
5.} As in Game 4, but we restore the prover \emph{PH}to compute proofs.
\emph{This is indistin-guishable by the unbounded zero-knowledge
property of the proof system.}

\textbf{Game 6.} Game 6 differs from the previous one in that the
decryption oracle restart to invoke Dec(\emph{ski, ·}) on input the
first ciphertext \emph{c}. \emph{This is indistinguishable due to the
soundness of the proof system.}

\begin{quote}
Note that this is exactly the experiment KDM-CCA in the case \emph{b} =
1.
\end{quote}

Denote by \emph{Si} the adversary success in Game \emph{i}, for \emph{i}
= 0\emph{,} 1\emph{, . . . ,} 6. Observe that
\emph{\textbar{}}Prob{[}\emph{S}0{]}
\emph{−}Prob{[}\emph{S}6{]}\emph{\textbar{}} bounds the advantage of a
KDM-CCA adversary attacking the scheme Π\emph{∗}. As in the proof of
Theorem 5, the difference \emph{\textbar{}}Prob{[}\emph{Si}{]}
\emph{−}Prob{[}\emph{Si}+1{]}\emph{\textbar{}} is negligible for all
\emph{i} = 1\emph{, . . .} 5, which makes negligible the advantage of a
KDM-CCA adversary against the scheme Π\emph{∗}. The only difference here
is in the scheme Π, that is KDM-CPA secure instead of simply CPA-secure.
In fact, the combined encryption scheme Π\emph{∗}inherits KDM-security
just from the scheme Π (when

